    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The recent advancements in lipid nanoparticle (LNP) formulation have significantly enhanced the stability and delivery efficiency of mRNA, primarily through the optimization of the ionizable lipid component's pKa value. By modulating the pKa of the headgroup, the hydrophilicity of the lipid bilayer is increased, thereby facilitating the interaction with the negatively charged endosomal membrane. This enhanced interaction enables the LNP to undergo a more efficient endosomal escape, a critical step in the delivery of mRNA to the cytosol. Specifically, the N/P ratio (nitrogen-to-phosphate) optimization strategy, which involves adjusting the stoichiometry of the cationic lipid and phosphate groups, has been shown to substantially augment protein expression in hepatic tissues. This is attributed to the increased efficiency of mRNA translation, which is mediated by the ribosome's ability to access the mRNA transcript in the cytosol.\n\nFurthermore, the incorporation of pseudouridine-substituted nucleosides into the mRNA backbone has been demonstrated to effectively mitigate the innate immune response, as mediated by TLR7 and TLR8 pathways. The pseudouridine modification reduces the recognition of the mRNA by pattern recognition receptors (PRRs), such as TLR7 and TLR8,",
      "source": "target",
